{
  "consensus": [
    "Malinowski's kula (1922) is the foundational ethnographic case, with soulava/mwali valuables circulating in opposite directions across 18 Massim island communities",
    "Mauss's tripartite obligation (give, receive, reciprocate) and the hau concept are canonical anchors, though Sahlins reinterprets it secularly as 'return on' or 'product of'",
    "Sahlins's three reciprocities (generalized, balanced, negative) indexed by social/kinship distance remain the dominant typology, with Graeber (in mimo) extending via 'everyday communism'",
    "Gregory's gift/commodity binary (1982) is the principal casualty of digital analysis—no digital phenomenon fits cleanly on either side",
    "Weiner's 'keeping-while-giving' and inalienable possessions (1976, 1992) plus the Weiner-Strathern disagreement define the feminist core",
    "All three contemporary domains (FOSS, attention economies, cryptocurrency) activate classical mechanisms but invert, hybridize, or extract value asymmetrically",
    "Cryptocurrency governance structures instantiate commons-based peer production principles (tokenization, decentralization, transparency) but reproduce plutocratic and gender-exclusionary dynamics"
  ],
  "contradictions": [
    {
      "issue": "Reinforcement vs. challenge to classical models",
      "positions": {
        "minimax-m3": "Differentiated: FOSS reinforces/modifies, attention economies strain, cryptocurrency is generative but Gregory's binary collapses",
        "glm-5.3-flash": "Strongly reinforcing—digital gifts are 'recognizably Maussian' with 'mechanized obligations to reciprocate'",
        "deepseek-v4-flash": "Hybrid regimes that 'selectively activate, distort, and monetize' classical mechanisms",
        "mimo-v2.5-pro": "Both: persistence of reciprocity/prestige dynamics alongside structural asymmetries that classical theory cannot contain"
      }
    },
    {
      "issue": "Whether FOSS is genuinely a gift economy",
      "positions": {
        "Raymond/Kelty/Zeitlyn": "Yes—gift culture with recursive public structure",
        "Petersen/250bpm (cited by deepseek)": "No—lacks three-party structure and obligation to receive; better described as 'social sharing'",
        "mimo": "Ambivalent—oligopolistic capture reproduces Weiner's 'withholding' dynamic"
      }
    },
    {
      "issue": "Whether digital attention economies constitute extraction or genuine reciprocity",
      "positions": {
        "Fourcade & Kluttz / Nachtwey & Schaupp": "Accumulation by gift—masks structural asymmetry and commodifies data",
        "Arvidsson/SAGE 'Economy of the Digital Gift'": "Genuine recognition-based exchange subsequently captured",
        "TikTok ethnographies": "Dual subjectification/objectification of influencers' virtual selves"
