  "final_guidance": "The four panels converge on a robust core narrative—Malinowski's kula, Mauss's tripartite obligation, Sahlins's reciprocity continuum, Weiner's inalienable wealth, Strathern's dividual—while diverging sharply on how to characterize digital extensions. Minimax-m3 offers the most theoretically layered synthesis with the widest theoretical interlocutors (Bourdieu, Derrida, Bataille, Ostrom); deepseek-v4-flash provides the most empirically grounded treatment with explicit gender statistics and cross-platform analysis; mimo-v2.5-pro uniquely extends the feminist critique through Wainberg and grounds crypto analysis in the Giveth DAO ethnography; glm-5.3-flash offers distinctive quantitative anchors but is truncated. For a comprehensive review, integrate: (1) minimax-m3's theoretical synthesis and unresolved-tensions framework, (2) deepseek-v4-flash's quantitative gender data and blockchain-rivalry argument, (3) mimo-v2.5-pro's Giveth DAO case and Graeber 'everyday communism' extension, (4) glm-5.3-flash's kula-as-emergent-order bridge and Maurer HAU reading. The strongest single synthesis position—drawing across all four—is that digital gift economies do not refute classical theory but rather expose its constitutive tensions: the hau was always already extractable, the dividual was always already commodifiable, and Gregory's binary was always already unstable. The contribution of contemporary ethnography is not to choose between 'reinforce' and 'challenge' but to specify the precise mechanisms by which gift logics are simultaneously activated and captured—a finding with strong empirical support across all three digital domains."
